    ('30000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000045', NULL, 'Instructions for Remembering Fire', $story$Celia arrives at a mountain archive expecting an ordinary day. Instead, celia discovers contradictory testimonies about a historic wildfire. The situation seems small at first, yet it interrupts the plans of several people and demands careful attention. With damaged cassette recordings close at hand, Celia decides to investigate before making a quick judgment.\n\nCelia meets ranger Idris, who understands part of the situation but not all of it. Together they agree to preserve disagreement without manufacturing consensus. They ask questions, inspect the available evidence, and consider who might be affected by their choices. Their first attempt fails, but the failure shows them where their assumptions were weak.\n\nThe controversy acquires its force from a paradox: the mechanisms designed to establish legitimacy have begun to manufacture the appearance of consent. Each archive is simultaneously evidence and intervention, preserving fragments while arranging them according to assumptions its custodians seldom acknowledge. Celia returns to the central problem with a clearer purpose, while ranger Idris tests a different possibility. The two approaches reveal details that neither person could have noticed alone.\n\nEfforts to neutralize ambiguity merely displace it into technical vocabulary, where political choices masquerade as inevitable procedure. The protagonists therefore confront not an absence of knowledge but an excess of incompatible knowledge, each account coherent within its own moral horizon. Their work attracts attention from others at a mountain archive, some helpful and some doubtful. Celia explains the evidence without exaggerating it and invites criticism before the group commits to a decision.\n\nTheir eventual compact remains deliberately provisional, binding authority to disclosure, contestability, and the possibility of principled reversal. In the end, the 